WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.079
This is Tanmay Kulkarni,
for Humanitarians AI.

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:06.528
Listen to one sentence,
inside a simulated cathedral.

00:00:07.558 --> 00:00:10.708
Every room you play in
is asking you a question.

00:00:13.808 --> 00:00:16.815
Hear how every word is still
ringing when the next one arrives?

00:00:16.855 --> 00:00:18.949
David Byrne, the singer
from Talking Heads,

00:00:18.969 --> 00:00:21.640
wrote a book about this,
called How Music Works.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.562
His big idea is that a room doesn't
just hold music. It shapes it.

00:00:26.066 --> 00:00:30.125
The way I'd put it: the room asks
a question, and the player answers.

00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:33.337
In 2015, two researchers
in Berlin measured

00:00:33.357 --> 00:00:35.953
how much one player
changes from room to room.

00:00:35.993 --> 00:00:38.352
The cellist Jean-Guihen
Queyras played all six

00:00:38.372 --> 00:00:41.266
of Bach's cello suites in
seven European concert halls.

00:00:41.306 --> 00:00:44.355
They measured how much each hall
echoed, and how fast he played.

00:00:44.395 --> 00:00:46.731
In the most echoing halls, he slowed down.

00:00:46.771 --> 00:00:51.300
Musicians have advised that since at
least 1752: give the echo time to clear.

00:00:51.791 --> 00:00:55.848
But in the driest halls, the ones with
almost no echo, he slowed down too.

00:00:55.888 --> 00:00:57.182
Too much echo, slower.

00:00:57.254 --> 00:00:58.960
Too little echo, also slower.

00:00:59.404 --> 00:01:04.157
One measure of the hall's echo accounted
for about 74% of how his tempo moved.

00:01:05.008 --> 00:01:08.850
Why both? In echoing halls, he told
them, he lets the pauses run longer.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:12.134
In dry halls, the researchers
think a player holds notes longer,

00:01:12.154 --> 00:01:14.391
to fill in for an echo that isn't there.

00:01:14.431 --> 00:01:16.460
Same answer. Opposite questions.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.890
Try it yourself first.
In a moment, clap once.

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:22.336
If the clap hangs in the
air, your room has echo.

00:01:22.376 --> 00:01:24.847
If it dies right away, it's dry. Go ahead.

00:01:27.183 --> 00:01:30.973
Now say the sentence, every room you
play in is asking you a question,

00:01:31.013 --> 00:01:33.131
at the pace your room seems to want.

00:01:36.392 --> 00:01:37.780
Did you speed up, or slow down?

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.064
A good answer names what your
room was doing. Here's mine.

00:01:41.104 --> 00:01:44.162
First, the cathedral, with
room between the phrases.

00:01:44.617 --> 00:01:50.657
Every room you play in
is asking you a question.

00:01:54.701 --> 00:01:58.054
And now, almost no echo at all,
holding the words longer.

00:01:58.884 --> 00:02:02.504
Every room you play in
is asking you a question.

00:02:03.217 --> 00:02:04.753
So a room doesn't write the music.

00:02:04.773 --> 00:02:07.241
It asks a question,
and the player answers.

00:02:07.281 --> 00:02:10.110
The full video tries that
on twelve more musicians,

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:12.031
a city full of birds, and the skip button.

00:02:12.071 --> 00:02:14.662
Tanmay Kulkarni, for Humanitarians AI.
